          }
        },
        {
          "id": "bottom",
          "label": "bottom",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "side": "bottom",
          "offset": 0.5,
          "position": {
            "side": "bottom",
            "offset": 0.5
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "left",
          "label": "left",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "side": "left",
          "offset": 0.5,
          "position": {
            "side": "left",
            "offset": 0.5
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "right",
          "label": "right",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "side": "right",
          "offset": 0.5,
          "position": {
            "side": "right",
            "offset": 0.5
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Codex: Wikipedia Article V1\n\nKeep the first Wikipedia node deliberately simple: **one node represents one Wikipedia article**.\n\nThe goal is not to reproduce the Wikipedia website inside a node. The goal is to represent an article as a useful graph-native object.\n\n### Authored state\n\nThe user should only need to provide an article reference, ideally `ref`.\n\n### Derived article state\n\nResolve and maintain:\n\n- `canonicalUrl`\n- `title`\n- `revisionId` or equivalent source-version identity\n- `lead`\n- `sections[]`\n- `attribution`\n\nDo not require the user to manually author resolved Wikipedia content.\n\n### Article structure\n\n`sections[]` is the structural manifest for the article. Each section entry should contain enough information to identify, label, order, and later resolve that section without eagerly storing every section body on the article node.\n\nA useful minimum section entry is:\n\n- stable `id`\n- visible `title`\n- `level`\n- `order`\n- source locator or equivalent resolver identity\n\n### Dynamic section handles\n\nDerive labeled expansion handles from `sections[]`.\n\nDragging or expanding a section handle should instantiate a `wikipedia-section` node representing that section. Section content may be resolved lazily when the section is instantiated.\n\nThe handles represent article structure, not duplicated section bodies.\n\n### Views\n\nSemantic views should progressively expose the same article:\n\n- glyph: Wikipedia identity\n- icon: recognizable Wikipedia article representation\n- summary: title plus concise lead content\n- detail: readable title, lead, source information, and useful article presentation\n\nSection handles are graph interaction and should remain available independently of how much prose a particular semantic view renders.\n\n### Data-model cleanup\n\nTreat `lead` and `sections[]` as the fundamental article content structure. Avoid making `firstSection` a required source-model concept. If a detail view wants to display the first section after the lead, derive that presentation from the ordered section manifest instead.\n\n### Scope for V1\n\nDo not expand the contract yet to model infoboxes, citations, categories, backlinks, Wikidata entities, edit history, galleries, tables, or every inline Wikipedia link.\n\nThose can be added later without weakening the basic article contract.\n\n### V1 success condition\n\nGiven one Wikipedia article reference, Twilite can resolve a recognizable, readable article node and expose its section structure as labeled graph expansion points. Expanding one of those points produces a useful Wikipedia section node.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "wikipedia-article-template-declaration"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1787602398463,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        }
